          <p>Comets are small icy bodies that release gas and dust as they approach the Sun, forming a bright coma and one or more tails. They originate in the Kuiper Belt and the Oort Cloud, relics of the Solar System's formation 4.6 billion years ago. Famous examples include Halley's Comet, Hale-Bopp, and Comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko.</p>
